Lab 10: Backup and Restore Control Plane
Objectives
Learn how to back up and restore key control plane components for disaster recovery.
Understand the importance of securing configuration files and testing restore procedures.
Prerequisites
A running OpenShift or Kubernetes cluster (Minikube, Kind, or OpenShift Local).
Administrative access to the cluster (kubectl or oc).
Basic knowledge of Linux commands.
etcdctl installed (for etcd backup/restore).
jq (for JSON processing).
Task 1: Back Up Databases and Message Queues
Subtask 1.1: Back Up etcd (Key-Value Store for Kubernetes)
Objective: Create a snapshot of the etcd database.

Access the etcd pod:

kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd
kubectl exec -it -n kube-system <etcd-pod-name> -- sh
Take an etcd snapshot:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
snapshot save /var/lib/etcd/snapshot.db
Verify the snapshot:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --write-out=table snapshot status /var/lib/etcd/snapshot.db
Copy the snapshot to a secure location:

kubectl cp -n kube-system <etcd-pod-name>:/var/lib/etcd/snapshot.db ~/backup/etcd-snapshot.db
Expected Outcome:

A snapshot of etcd is saved and verified.
Troubleshooting:

If etcdctl is not found, install it using:
sudo apt-get install etcd-client
Subtask 1.2: Back Up OpenShift/Kubernetes Message Queues (Kafka/RabbitMQ if used)
Objective: Back up message queue data if the cluster uses Kafka or RabbitMQ.

Check if Kafka/RabbitMQ is running:

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> | grep -E "kafka|rabbitmq"
Back up Kafka topics (if applicable):

kubectl exec -it <kafka-pod> -- /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh --list --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 > ~/backup/kafka-topics.txt
Back up RabbitMQ definitions (if applicable):

kubectl exec -it <rabbitmq-pod> -- rabbitmqadmin export ~/backup/rabbitmq-definitions.json
Expected Outcome:

A backup of message queue configurations is stored.
Task 2: Store and Secure Configuration Files
Subtask 2.1: Back Up Kubernetes/OpenShift Configuration Files
Objective: Secure critical configuration files.

Back up kubeconfig and cluster manifests:

cp ~/.kube/config ~/backup/kubeconfig-backup
kubectl get all --all-namespaces -o yaml > ~/backup/cluster-state.yaml
Back up Persistent Volume (PV) and StorageClass definitions:

kubectl get pv,pvc,sc -A -o yaml > ~/backup/storage-backup.yaml
Encrypt the backup files (Optional but recommended):

tar -czvf backup.tar.gz ~/backup/
gpg --output backup.tar.gz.gpg --encrypt --recipient <your-email> backup.tar.gz
Expected Outcome:

Critical configuration files are backed up and optionally encrypted.
Task 3: Test Restore Procedures and Validate Functionality
Subtask 3.1: Restore etcd from Snapshot
Objective: Validate the etcd restore process.

Stop the kube-apiserver:

sudo systemctl stop kube-apiserver
Restore etcd from snapshot:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
snapshot restore /var/lib/etcd/snapshot.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd/restored
Update etcd configuration to point to the restored data:

sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
Modify --data-dir to /var/lib/etcd/restored.
Restart kube-apiserver:

sudo systemctl start kube-apiserver
Expected Outcome:

The cluster should come back online with the restored state.
Troubleshooting:

If the API server fails to start, check logs:
journalctl -u kube-apiserver -f
Subtask 3.2: Validate Cluster Functionality Post-Restore
Objective: Ensure the restored cluster is functional.

Check cluster status:

kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A
Test a sample application:

kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80
kubectl get svc nginx
Expected Outcome:

The cluster should respond normally, and applications should run as expected.
Conclusion
Successfully backed up etcd, message queues, and configuration files.
Tested and validated the restore process.
Learned best practices for disaster recovery in Kubernetes/OpenShift.
Next Steps:

